<h2 id="distribution-theory">Distribution Theory ◆</h2>
<p>With distribution theory one extends the concept of functions to include objects, known as <em>distributions</em> or <em>generalized functions</em>, which can be used to rigorously define operations like differentiation even for functions that are not classically differentiable. This theory is particularly useful in handling singularities or discontinuities, such as the Dirac delta function, which models an infinitely concentrated point of mass or charge. Distribution theory provides a powerful framework for solving partial differential equations, e.g., using Green’s functions.</p>
<p>A rudimentary knowledge of distribution theory is very useful in the study of quantum mechanics. The standard informal view is that “the Dirac delta is a function which is infinite everywhere except at a single point where it is infinity”, similarly that “the Green’s function is the response of the system to a Dirac delta function”, is very useful, but will only take one so far.</p>
<p>Useful in (for example): - Response theory - Manybody Green’s function theory - Electromagnetism</p>
